## 1. INTRODUCTION
Although most materials display positive thermal expansion (PTE) due to the inherent anharmonicity of bond potentials,1-3 some display the anomalous properties of negative thermal expansion (NTE) or near-zero thermal expansion (ZTE).4-6 In principle, NTE materials can be used to compensate for the PTE of a matrix resulting in a controlled thermal expansion composite.7-8 NTE in open framework materials9-10 such as metal oxides,1-3, 6, 11 metal fluorides/oxyfluorides,12-14 cyanides,15-19 and metal-organic frameworks (MOFs),20-25 typically arises from the presence of low frequency vibrational modes that soften on volume reduction.9-10 Strategies for controlling NTE in a given framework, such as the formation of solid solutions or the insertion of guest molecules,2, 26-28 can be thought of as methods for modifying the vibrational modes and their response to volume change.
MOFs, unlike typical framework solids, offer structure types more amenable to systematic change due to their modular buildup.29 Construction of countless MOF topologies, using  different metals (or metal clusters) and organic ligands, is possible.30 The preparation of solid solutions incorporating different ligands or metal clusters in a singlephase material enables fine tuning of material properties, including thermal expansion.14,31-38 Although NTE has been reported in a number of $\mathsf{M O F}s^{21,39}$ , studies leveraging the modular build up and controlled exchangeability of building blocks, or the preparation of solid solutions, to manipulate thermal behavior are scarce.40-41 To our knowledge there is no experimental evidence of tunable thermal expansion from negative to positive via the formation of MOF solid solutions in the peer-reviewed scientific literature, although there have been reports where the magnitude of thermal expansion has been tuned by incorporating guest molecules, isovalent metals, and structural defects.28, 41-43
Zn-DMOF, $\mathrm{Zn_{2}(b d c)_{2}(d a b c o)_{\it1}}$ , is the parent framework of an anisotropic pillared-layered MOF family whose members are known to exhibit NTE in some directions and PTE in others.44-45 Zn-DMOF is tetragonal and contains $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}_{2}$ paddlewheels connected in their equatorial plane by 1,4-benzenedicarboxylate $(\mathrm{bdc}^{2\cdot})$ forming $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{bdc})_{2}$ layers that exhibit NTE. These layers are connected by 1,4-diazabicyclo[2.2.2]octane (dabco), which forms a pillar connecting the planes to one another (see Figure 1). Studies examining the flexibility, water stability, and thermal expansion of ZnDMOF, and related functionalized forms, have been reported in the literature.46-52 The Zn-DMOF platform is capable of accommodating a range of ligand substitutions, including TM-bdc2- (2,3,5,6-tetramethyl-1,4-benzenedicarboxylate), which forms a compound isostructural to the parent framework, denoted Zn-DMOF-TM $\mathrm{\Delta}Z{\mathrm{n}}_{2}(\mathrm{TM}-\$ bdc)2(dabco)).53 A solid solution containing equal parts of TM-bdc2- and bdc2- was recently reported,53 suggesting that solid solution formation may be a viable approach for tuning the thermal expansion coefficients between the values of the parent end members Zn-DMOF and Zn-DMOF-TM. In this contribution, we report the preparation of $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{bdc})_{2}$ - $\scriptstyle2\mathrm{x}(\mathrm{TM-bdc})_{2\mathrm{x}}(\mathrm{dabco})$ $(\mathbf{x}~=~0.0$ , 0.17, 0.45, 0.67, and 1.0), herein termed $\mathbf{Zn{\mathrm{-}}D M O F{\mathrm{-}}T M_{x}}$ and show that the TMbdc/bdc ratio in the solid solution has a marked effect on thermal expansion properties of the framework.
## 2. EXPERIMENTAL
2.1. Syntheses. $\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\bullet}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ $(98\%)$ , 1,4-benzenedicarboxylic acid (H2bdc) $(98\%)$ , and 1,4-diazabicyclo[2.2.2]octane (dabco) $(>99\%)$ were purchased from Sigma Aldrich. 2,3,5,6-tetramethyl-1,4-benzenedicarboxylate (H2TM-bdc) $(98.1\%)$ was purchased from CHEM SERVICE, inc. All chemicals were used without further purification.
Similar to the reported literature procedure for ZnDMOF, Zn-DMOF-TMx solid solutions were formed by dissolving $\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0.$ , dabco, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{bdc}$ , and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{TM}$ -bdc in dimethylformamide (DMF) in varying molar amounts.46 As an example, Zn-DMOF-TM0.45 was synthesized using $0.180\ {\mathrm{g}}$ $\left(6.05\mathrm{mmol}\right)$ of $\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\bullet}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ , $0.050{\mathrm{g}}$ $\mathsf{3.01m m o l}_{.}^{\cdot}$ ) H2bdc, 0.067 g (3.01 mmol) $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{TM}$ -bdc, $0.036\mathrm{g}$ $3.20\mathrm{mmol}\cdot$ ) dabco, and $9~\mathrm{mL}$ of DMF. The initially formed solution was sonicated to help ensure complete dissolution. Upon standing a turbid suspension formed. The white colloid was separated via centrifugation. The remaining clear solution was placed into a glass vial, capped and heated at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 48 hours. The resulting large clear crystals were washed by replacing the mother liquor with dry DMF for one hour and then further washed/solvent exchanged with dry chloroform three times over the course of three days. Each solid solution sample was dried under nitrogen flow before drying at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 10 hours under vacuum.
2.2. X-ray Powder Diffraction. Data were collected at beamline 11-ID-B of the Advanced Photon Source, Argonne National Laboratory, using a wavelength of $\lambda=0.2\bar{11}3\mathrm{~\AA~}$ . Powder diffraction patterns were recorded at a sample to detector distance of $1000\ \mathrm{mm}$ on a Perkin Elmer amorphous silicon 2D detector. The samples were mounted in borosilicate capillaries under helium flow, using a previously described flow cell.54 The sample temperature was controlled using an Oxford Cryosystems Cryostream.
2.3. Porosity and Surface Area Measurements. Porosity measurements were performed using a Micromeritics ASAP 2020 instrument. $50{-}100\mathrm{mg}$ of each sample was activated at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 10 hours before the collection of nitrogen isotherms at $77\mathrm{K}.$ .
2.4. NMR measurements. 1H-NMR measurements were performed using a Bruker $500^{\prime}54$ Ascend $(500~\mathrm{MHz})$ using solutions containing approximately $5~\mathrm{mg}$ of each sample dissolved in $0.5\mathrm{mL}$ of DMSO-d6 and $50\upmu\mathrm{l}$ of $\mathrm{D}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ .
2.5. IR measurements. Spectra where collected using an Agilent Technologies Cary-630, equipped with an ATR accessory. The measurements were performed in an Argon filled glovebox to minimize noise. A background spectrum was collected prior to each measurement.
## 3. RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
3.1 Sample Purity, Composition, and Porosity. Powder X-ray diffraction patterns collected using synchrotron-radiation (Fig. 2) indicate that all prepared compositions (Zn$\mathsf{D M O F-T M}_{\mathrm{x}_{\mathrm{\ell}}}$ ; $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{x}}=0.0$ , 0.17, 0.45, 0.67, and 1.0) crystallize in the tetragonal space group $P4/m m m$ at room temperature. For Zn-DMOF a small impurity peak at $2\theta=1.48^{\circ}$ was present. However, the size of this reflection, and the absence of any other significant reflections corresponding to any impurity, suggest that the thermal expansion of the main phase can be studied without interference from the impurity. The sample’s room temperature a-axis lattice constant, obtained using Le Bail fits in GSAS-II,55 change in an approximately linear fashion over the composition range examined while the c-axis displays linearity from $\mathbf{x}{=}.17$ to $\mathrm{x}{=}1.0$ (Figure S1). Tabulated lattice constants and representative fits to the diffraction data (Figure S2) are available in the Supporting Information.
The 1H NMR and IR spectra for each material indicate that the samples contain no significant non-crystalline impurities or remnant solvent from the syntheses (Figures $S3\ \&$ S4). The ratio of the two ligands in the solid solution samples was controlled by changing the relative amounts of H2bdc and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{TM}$ -bdc used in the syntheses. Figure 3 compares the H2bdc to $\mathtt{H}_{2}\mathtt{T M}$ -bdc ratio used in the syntheses with that incorporated into the MOF, as ascertained by $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ - NMR on dissolved powder and single crystal samples of the MOF solid solution. The $\mathbf{x}=0.45$ powder and the $\mathbf{x}=0.43$ single crystal data points in this figure are from the same synthesis batch. The results shown in Figure 3 indicate that the ratio of linker units incorporated into the MOF is close to that used in the syntheses and that the ratio incorporated into a single crystal is close to that of bulk powder made under the same conditions. ${\sf N}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms were collected at $77\mathrm{K}$ to assess the porosity of the samples. The BET surface area for the Zn-DMOF sample was $2119\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ which is consistent with previous reports.56 A decrease in both pore volume and BET surface area (Figure 4) was observed with increasing incorporation of the bulkier TM-bdc2- ligand. For the samples Zn-DMOF-TM0.17, Zn-DMOF-TM0.45, Zn-DMOF-TM0.67 and Zn-DMOF-TM1.0 the BET surface areas were 1743, 1444, 1280, and $1067~\mathrm{{m^{2}g^{-1}}}$ , respectively, and the nitrogen uptakes were 430, 369, 337 and $305\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-}$ 1 at $p/p_{0}=1$ .
In contrast to the behavior in the $\scriptstyle a-b$ plane, the thermal expansion parallel to the crystallographic c-axis is positive for all compositions (Figure S5). Linear CTEs, estimated from $2^{\mathrm{nd}}$ order polynomials fitted to the lattice constants as a function of temperature (Figure S7), are strongly positive across the entire sample series at all temperatures, with average values falling in the range $15\cdot21\mathrm{\ppm/K}$ . The response of the thermal expansion in the $c$ -axis to changes in composition are not surprising, as the methyl groups on the TM-bdc ligands have been previously shown via solid-state NMR and molecular simulations to have minimal interaction with the pillaring dabco ligands.57 Four of the five compositions studied exhibit ZTE along the a/b-axes in the temperature range studied. The temperature at which zero thermal expansion occurs changes in an approximately linear manner with sample composition, indicating near-direct tunability of the phenomenon (Figure 6).
## 4. CONCLUSIONS
Single-phase mixed linker Zn-DMOF- $\mathbf{TM}_{\mathbf{x}}$ solid solutions were prepared using a simple solvothermal synthetic approach across the composition range $0<\mathbf{x}<1.{}^{1}\mathrm{H}$ NMR spectroscopy showed an almost linear relationship between the ratio of the two linkers, $\operatorname{bdc}^{2-}$ and TM-bdc2- in the reaction mixture and the final solid solution products. The pore volume, BET surface area, and room temperature lattice constants vary smoothly with composition. As the composition is changed, the thermal expansion in the a-b plane evolves from predominantly negative for $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{x}}=0$ to positive for $\mathbf{\deltax}=1.0$ . This high level of tunability for the Zn-DMOF- $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{TM_{x}}$ system suggests that the systematic substitution of functionalized ligands into other MOF frameworks may provide access to many different families of controlled thermal expansion materials. While many other MOFs are known to accept functionalized versions of their organic linkers,31 at this time, there is little experimental or computational evidence regarding how this affects their thermal behavior.
